$$\frac{dS}{dt} = (\text{Rate of salt in}) - (\text{Rate of salt out})$$

1. **Rate of salt in:**
The concentration of salt in the incoming water is $C_{in}(t) = \frac{1}{4}\left(1+\frac{1}{2} \sin t\right) \mathrm{oz} / \mathrm{gal}$.
The inflow rate is $R_{in} = 2 \mathrm{gal} / \mathrm{min}$.
$$\text{Rate of salt in} = C_{in}(t) \cdot R_{in} = \frac{1}{4}\left(1+\frac{1}{2} \sin t\right) \cdot 2 = \frac{1}{2}\left(1+\frac{1}{2} \sin t\right) = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{4} \sin t \quad \text{oz/min}$$

2. **Rate of salt out:**
The concentration of salt in the tank at time $t$ is $C_{out}(t) = \frac{S(t)}{V} = \frac{S(t)}{100} \mathrm{oz} / \mathrm{gal}$.
The outflow rate is $R_{out} = 2 \mathrm{gal} / \mathrm{min}$.
$$\text{Rate of salt out} = C_{out}(t) \cdot R_{out} = \frac{S(t)}{100} \cdot 2 = \frac{S(t)}{50} \quad \text{oz/min}$$

